## 2026 in Recalls: By the Numbers

Recalls show no sign of slowing down, as we edge towards the year's midway point with 433 recalls already logged. That's more than a recall a day, folks. Of these, 105 are classified as Class I--the FDA's sternest warning of products that can cause serious health consequences or death. To illustrate, 246 of the total recalls were due to allergens, 114 to salmonella risks, and 91 stemmed from foreign objects--ranging from glass shards to metallic flakes.

## Recent Headlines: Dakota Honey Company Spoils the Pot

Dakota Honey Company accounts for a quartet of the latest recalls. Their salted caramel, jalapeno-infused, cinnamon, and sampler pack honeys were recued over contamination with stainless steel dust and plastic shreds. Stir before using, they suggested--perhaps to mix the debris in evenly?

## Repeat Offenders: Flunking the Safety Course

Among this year's repeat offenders, a few brands have managed to distinguish themselves--for all the wrong reasons:

1. Moonlight Packing Corporation: 11 recalls this year, all Class I. Grade: F.
2. Albertsons Companies LLC: 11 recalls this year, 10 Class I. Grade: F.
3. CHETAK NEW YORK LLC: 52 recalls, all Class I over their history. Grade: D (and that's generous).
4. Diva Fam Inc: 14 recalls this year, but somehow managed to avoid a single Class I. Grade: F.

## Lessons from the Croutons

The only Class I recall of the latest batch was Sugar Foods LLC's Kroger Homestyle Cheese and Garlic Croutons. Salmonella concerns stemming from their supplier's non-fat milk powder underscores the domino effect of failures throughout the supply chain. Just another reminder to trust, but verify. Or in this case, trust, and get a second opinion.
